# apatch conformance (does the whole contract still hold)

Opt-in: inert unless `.apatch/conformance.json` has `enabled: true`. apatch gives the
lever; the **project** decides what to enforce — never turn on blocking yourself.

## When to use

| Intent | Command |
|--------|---------|
| Is the contract still holding? | `apatch conformance status` (add `--json`) |
| Truthful check (run verifies now) | `apatch conformance status --live` |
| Gate a change | `apatch conformance gate --live --base <ref>` |
| CI gate (no local ledger) | `apatch conformance gate --live --ci-safe --base <ref>` |

## The four buckets — never conflate stale and drifted

- `conformant` — attested + verify green now.
- **`drifted`** — verify **red now** → real regression → blocks (default).
- `stale` — attested, attestation stale, verify still **green** → advisory; fix with
  the MCP tool `apatch_rebind_stale(spec='SPEC-X')`, do **not** treat as a bug.
- `unproven` — no real `verify` → a visible hole.

A stale attestation with a green verify is **bookkeeping, not a regression**. Only a
live red verify is blocking. Treating stale as drifted = the gate cries wolf.

## Levers (config — the project's choice, not yours)

`mode` (advisory|blocking) · `live_verify` · `ci_safe` (block only on self-contained
pytest) · `block_on` (which buckets block; default `["drifted"]`) · `contract` (scope).
`--base REF` makes live verify baseline-aware (only re-run changed specs).

## Never

- Flip `mode: blocking` or add buckets to `block_on` on your own — that's the owner's call.
- Re-attest a `stale` spec to hide a `drifted` one — run the verify, see which it is.
- Run a full live gate on a large contract without `--base` (it re-runs everything).
